    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records for polar nucleosides (lamivudine, ribavirin, didanosine, uridine, levovirin) show rapid absorption (tmax ~0.56–1.26 h) and measurable Cmax/AUC after oral dosing, consistent with oral absorption.",
      "card_ids": ["C86","C89","C96","C104","C105","C109","C110","C112","C120","C123","C130","C99","C93","C114","C84","C117"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 records show low passive permeability for several nucleoside analogs (ribavirin Caco-2 0.18×10^-6, levovirin 0.12×10^-6, gemcitabine 0.5–0.99×10^-6, didanosine PAMPA 0.11×10^-6, lamivudine Caco-2 ~0.3×10^-6 cm/s), but high aqueous solubility for ribavirin, lamivudine, and didanosine; low permeability alone is not affirmative F<20% evidence because direct F for didanosine/emtricitabine/ddA is >=20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C135","C162","C153","C160","C157","C159","C167","C174","C142","C138","C132","C140","C158","C145","C149","C76","C28","C22"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The only low-F record, PMEA (C63), is a charged nucleotide phosphonate with low transferability to guanosine.",
      "card_ids": ["C63"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No guanosine-specific direct F or permeability record is supplied; transfer from analogs is indirect and structural differences may alter absorption and first-pass metabolism.",
      "card_ids": []
    }
  ],
